# Working in this project

This is an **nr-workbench** project for neutron reflectometry analysis at
{{ facility }} {{ instrument }}. It was scaffolded by `nrw init`.

This file is the tool-neutral one. `AGENTS.md` is read directly by OpenCode and
by GitHub Copilot, and imported by `CLAUDE.md` for Claude Code, so there is one
copy of what follows rather than one per assistant. The shared engineering
workflow lives in `.github/copilot-instructions.md`.

## Read this first

**Before acting on a request that touches data, models, fits or reports,
establish two things: which project, and which sample.** Both come from a
command's output, never from an assumption, and neither
`nrw init` nor `nrw sample new` is yours to run without asking — `init` writes
about seventy files into whatever directory you are standing in, and a sample
created under the wrong id becomes the directory name in every later result
path. `skills/reflectometry/nrw-preflight/SKILL.md` is the standard; it is
short, and it exists because improvising around a missing project or a
misspelt sample produces work that looks fine and carries no provenance.

**If this project already has fits in it, start with one command:**

```bash
nrw handoff <sample>
```

It prints the whole state of play — the escalations a previous session left,
how that session ended, the integrity check, what has been fitted, who is
reading, and what to read in what order. It exists because rebuilding that
picture by hand has been measured at 28 tool calls, and everything in it is
derivable. `skills/reflectometry/analyst-handoff/SKILL.md` is the standard for
what to do with it.

If `nrw` is not found, the handoff tells you how to invoke it here. **Do not go
looking for the binary** — that search has cost ten tool calls before now.

`skills/reflectometry/nr-workbench-project/SKILL.md` is the map of this project:
where files belong, which command does what, and the provenance rules. Read it
before creating a file or running a fit.

## Skills

Skills live in [`skills/`](skills/) at the repository root — tool-neutral, so
every assistant reaches them by `Read`. None of them auto-discovers that folder,
so each skill has a **thin dispatcher agent** in {{ agent_dirs_prose }} whose
only job is to load the skill.

**To change a standard, edit its `SKILL.md` — never the dispatcher.**

`nrw skills sync` reinstalls the bundled skills after a package upgrade. A
skill you have edited locally is never silently replaced — the new version is
written alongside as `.nrw-new` for you to merge.

## Provenance rules

These are what make this project different from a folder of scripts. Breaking
them is how you end up unable to say which script produced a figure.

1. **Never hand-edit a generated fit script.** `models/<name>.py` is derived
   from `models/<name>.yaml`. Edit the spec and regenerate. If the spec cannot
   express what you need, run `nrw model fork` — that keeps full provenance.
2. **Never overwrite a result.** Every fit writes a new immutable directory
   under `samples/<id>/results/<fit_id>/`.
3. **No absolute paths in committed files.** Everything resolves from the
   project root.
4. **No boolean toggles inside a fit script.** Two variants means two specs,
   each with its own hash and its own records.
5. **Run `nrw check` before calling anything final.**

## The person's commands

These change what the project claims, or send it somewhere else, so they are
the person's to run. The project's hook refuses them to you in Claude Code and
OpenCode, and `nrw` refuses them itself in an unattended session
(`NRW_AGENT=1`). Give the person the command and your reasons; unattended,
write both in `ESCALATIONS.md` and stop.

- `nrw promote`, and curating fits: `nrw fit star`, `unstar`, `discard`,
  `restore` and `delete`
- `nrw isaac push`, even with `--validate-only`, and `nrw isaac export --upload`
- `nrw aure run` (with `--dry-run` it is yours: it only validates the setup)
- `nrw experiment assign` and `release`, and `apply`, `adopt` or `settings`
  with `--write` (without it they only preview, and are yours)
- `nrw sample reset` (with `--dry-run` it is yours)
- `nrw init --nested`, and any `--force`
- every `nrw tiled` command, and Tiled's own `tiled login`, `tree` and the
  rest: each acts with the person's login, and a push publishes this
  project's claims to everyone on the experiment

`nrw agent guard --command '<command>'` says whether a command would be
refused, and why.

## Write down what you learn, next to what it is about

The analysis is the thinking, not the fits. Record it as you go, scoped to what
it is about:

| A finding about… | Goes in | Written with |
|---|---|---|
| one run | `samples/<id>/results/<fit_id>/NOTES.md` | `nrw note <fit_id> -m "..."` |
| how the fits relate | `samples/<id>/reports/*.md` | `nrw note --sample <id> --title "..." -m "..."` |
| the tooling, the instrument, a project-wide convention | [`docs/ground_truths.md`](docs/ground_truths.md) | an editor |

**If a finding names a run number or a fit id, it belongs to the sample** — not
to `docs/ground_truths.md`, which is for what is true regardless of which
sample you are looking at.

### `sample.md` is the scientist's file. Do not write to it.

`samples/<id>/sample.md` is the one file in this project an assistant must not
edit. It is the live record of an experiment in progress, updated by hand as
measurements happen, and it is the *input* to everything else — `nrw aure new`
and `nrw model new --from-notes` both build the model from it.

Read it, quote it, build specs from it. Do not write to it. An assistant
editing it is competing with the person who holds the knowledge, and can
silently overwrite something they added a minute ago.

A `sample.md` that opens with *"Written from the experiment catalog"* is
rendered from `experiment/*.parquet`, which the scientist edits on the
Experiment page (`nrw serve`). It is still not yours to edit -- and there a
hand edit does not even reach the catalog: it only stops the next apply from
updating the file.

Which sample a run belongs to, under what condition, whether it is used, and
where the data is read from are the scientist's decisions too. Read them with
`nrw experiment status --json`. `nrw experiment assign` and `release`, and
`apply`, `adopt` or `settings` with `--write`, are the person's commands (see
"The person's commands" above): propose them instead.

That includes the sections that look like invitations. If `## Description` is
empty and `nrw aure new` refuses, or the six questions in
`skills/reflectometry/aure-first-fit/SKILL.md` have no answers in there, that
is **a blocker to raise, not a gap to fill.** Ask, and say exactly what is
missing.

Everything an assistant learns has somewhere else to go — the three rows of the
table above. Use them.

Cite fit ids in the prose. That is the whole linking mechanism: `nrw ls`, the
fit page and `nrw pack` all find a report by the ids it mentions.

After a fit, `nrw assess <fit_id>` writes the automatic checks — parameters on
their bounds, unconstrained posteriors, BIC — into its `NOTES.md`, and adds a
language model's reading if an endpoint is configured. Run it, then add the
sentence it cannot write: what you were testing, and what you now believe.

**A fit you are abandoning still needs a note saying why.** That sentence is
worth more than the fit and is the first thing forgotten.

## Commands

**Setting up and looking around**

| Task | Command |
|---|---|
| **Take over an analysis in progress** | `nrw handoff <ID>` |
| Check the environment | `nrw doctor` |
| Make bare `nrw` work in assistant sessions | `nrw doctor --fix-path` |
| Say who is reading, so reports are pitched right | `nrw audience --ask` |
| Add a sample | `nrw sample new <ID>` |
| See what data is actually on disk | `nrw sample scan <ID>` |
| See every run of the experiment, and its sample | `nrw experiment status --json` |
| Preview what applying the catalog would write | `nrw experiment apply` |
| Browse everything in a browser | `nrw serve` |

**Understanding the data before modelling it**

| Task | Command |
|---|---|
| Assess a tNR run | `nrw tnr assess data/tnr/<dir> --label <label>` |
| One metric at a time | `nrw tnr amplitude\|variogram\|chi2\|qbands\|pca <dir>` |

Read the `assessment.json` verdict before choosing a constraint form — it
reports whether anything is changing, whether one reaction coordinate explains
it, and whether the change looks like a thickness or a contrast.

**A first fit, when there is no model yet**

`nrw aure` hands the sample to AuRE, which proposes a layer stack from a
plain-English description and iterates it against the data. Read
`skills/reflectometry/aure-first-fit/SKILL.md` first — it is mostly about the
six questions to ask the scientist, because AuRE builds the whole model from
their answers and nothing in the data supplies them.

| Task | Command |
|---|---|
| Write the setup from the data and `sample.md` | `nrw aure new <ID> --run <run>` |
| Check the setup without running it | `nrw aure run <setup.yaml> --dry-run` |
| Run it (needs an LLM endpoint; the person's command) | `nrw aure run <setup.yaml>` |
| ...and retry a thin layer in the wrong basin | `nrw aure run <setup.yaml> --mode-enumeration` |
| Turn the fitted model into a model spec | `nrw aure import <output> --sample <ID> --name <name>` |

**An AuRE run is reconnaissance, not a result.** It carries no fit record, so
the run that counts is the `nrw fit run` on the script generated from the
imported spec.

**Modelling**

`nrw model new` fills in the facts — runs and files. It writes no angles: each
one is read from its file's header when the spec is resolved. It cannot fill in
the stack. Run it with `--print-prompt` to get the exact instruction for that,
including which skills to read; `--from-notes` does the same thing against a
configured LLM endpoint.

Whichever route: **never edit `states`, `series`, `thetas`, `data_dir` or
`run`, and never add an angle.** A `null` angle is one that no file records. It
is for the person who ran the measurement to give: ask them, and never fill it
in from habit or from another run. A guessed angle sets the wavelength axis, and
the fit absorbs the error into roughness rather than reporting it.

| Task | Command |
|---|---|
| Start a spec from what the scan found | `nrw model new <ID> --name <name>` |
| ...and let an LLM propose the stack | `nrw model new ... --from-notes` |
| ...or get the instruction to do it yourself | `nrw model new ... --print-prompt` |
| Check a spec without fitting | `nrw model validate <spec.yaml>` |
| Build it in-process and report the parameter table | `nrw model preview <spec.yaml>` |
| Write the refl1d script | `nrw model generate <spec.yaml>` |
| Take manual ownership of a spec's generated script | `nrw model fork <spec.yaml>` |

**Fitting and provenance**

| Task | Command |
|---|---|
| Run a fit (spec-generated or hand-written) | `nrw fit run <script.py>` -- DREAM unless `--method` or nrw.toml `[fit]` says otherwise |
| List fits | `nrw ls` |
| Trace a figure, `.par`, or data file to its fit | `nrw whence <path>` |
| Compare two fits | `nrw diff <fit_a> <fit_b>` |
| Bless a result -- the person's command: give it to them | `nrw promote <fit_id> --as final --reason '...'`, or **Finalize** on the fit's page in `nrw serve` |
| Star a fit, set one aside, or delete a discarded one's files -- the person's too | `nrw fit star <fit_id>`, `nrw fit unstar <fit_id>`, `nrw fit discard <fit_id> --reason '...'`, `nrw fit restore <fit_id>`, `nrw fit delete <fit_id>` |
| Verify project integrity | `nrw check` |

`nrw serve` also exposes `/api/…`, which returns exactly what the browser
renders — useful for reading a curve, an assessment, or a fit's provenance
without opening a page. `/api/experiment` reads the same way; its write
endpoints accept only a browser that opened the link `nrw serve` printed, and
reaching for them is the same decision as `nrw experiment apply --write`.

Run `nrw --help` for the full surface.
